A||||43||GT Bastard||Observations of little consequence...  please take a moment with me||||3||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||11-10-2001||05:44 AM||||It's 4:30am and I'm still not tired.  <P>As I was driving around today I was thinking about war/death/destruction and what people in and around Afghanistan were thinking, and then I noticed a sign that caught my eye.  It was in front of a gas station, and it read: "GOD BLESS AMERICA / NACHOS $1.39"  Ahhh, what a crazy world we live in.  We drop over 8,000 bombs on another country, but we can still get our nacho fix here in the USA. I can watch these poor bastards die on t.v. in the privacy of my own home.  It might as well be a movie.  Explosions and death.... Ho-hum, I've seen it all before. I've watched Saving Private Ryan, Terminator, and countless other violent movies.  Nothing surprises me anymore.  And so I sit with my mammoth Cafe Mocha w/3 shots of expresso and wonder what it's like to TRUELY care what's going on around me.  <P>ps- This is where someone responds to this post with something lame like: "I like sour cream on my nachos."<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/icare.jpg"> ||||reg||3
Z||000001||Philphine||11-10-2001||06:49 AM||||i think the medical term for this comfortably numb.<P>and i don't really like sour cream.||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||11-10-2001||07:43 AM||||That post makes a lot of sense.We have become so used to fake death that the real thing doesn't bother us as it should.I guess the only time it bothers many of us is if it strikes close to home.||||reg||9
Z||000003||bHooper||11-10-2001||07:50 AM||||i don't like sour cream either, but i perfer jalapeno's on them.<P>so, how were the nacho's?!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||11-10-2001||08:38 AM||||I think the answer is that you havent seen real death, you have seen buildings blown up and bombs exploding, but you havent seen the bodies and the carnage.<P> If you saw what happens to a real person when they are blown in half, or if you saw body parts with your own eyes - and caught wind of the smell of death - if you had to scrap someones internal organs off the street with a shovel<P> then I dont think you would ever feel comfortable again.||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||11-10-2001||10:48 AM||||There goes my breakfast...||||reg||1
Z||000006||TRiAD||11-10-2001||11:14 AM||||What an interesting thread.<P>Good points, all.<P>Ken is absolutely right, I have been close to a very small amount of true horror, and it will last me a lifetime. I don' know how paramedics/doctors do it. Just remember that it is real, people are not just dying, but <I>hurting</I>. People are losing wives, husbands, mothers, fathers, siblings, children and cousins. Lives have already been irreparably and permanently changed. Make no mistake, there are people over there who see no right or good in what we're doing...we are making new enemies by the minute. <P>This sounds like I don't support our actions, but in fact I do. We just cannot for an instant convince ourselves in our ignorance than once we drop the last bomb and decide to go home that it's over. We don't have any say in when it's over, only on when we stop this current action. There will be repercussions, and we should damn well be ready for them next time. This was all started because of repercussions from the Gulf War, after all. (They were mad at us for inhabiting their "holy land").<P>I do understand what you're saying though...it can be easy to go through this like a sheltered American. <P>...Just don't get too carried away with those Jalepenos. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/85-Sig-2.gif">              <BR><B><I><u><FONT COLOR="red">TRiAD Motorsports</FONT></u></B> \\\\ <B>triadtuning@hotmail.com</B> //// Michael ~ 85 GT 4sp</I>||||reg||1
Z||000007||avengador1||11-10-2001||12:19 PM||||Real death is not a pretty site. <A HREF="http://www.rotten.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rotten.com</A>  will fix you.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||BN Boomer||11-10-2001||12:23 PM||||Maybe you should think about what those f*ckers where doing while the people they give shelter to were busy flying airplanes full of our people into the WTC and the Pentagon.<P>Maybe they were looking at a sign that read "Allah bless Afghanistan / Bean curd for 2 pieces of camel dung"<p>[This message has been edited by BN Boomer (edited 11-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Mach10||11-10-2001||12:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BN Boomer:<BR><B>Maybe you should think about what those f*ckers where doing while the people they give shelter to were busy flying airplanes full of our people into the WTC and the Pentagon.<P>Maybe they were looking at a sign that read "Allah bless Afghanistan / Bean curd for 2 pieces of camel dung"<P>[This message has been edited by BN Boomer (edited 11-10-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well that's just it, isn't it? You're right, and they are wrong. The US is busy hammering the people responsible. So far, civilian casualties (afghanis) have been minimal, while the Taliban losses (predominantly Pakistani) have been high. It's not so bad shooting a soldier. He/She knows why he/she's there. They are ready to die, and to kill. It's the civilian who is the innocent. <P>I can still empathize with the Taliban soldier watching the 2000lb bomb getting much bigger, really fast. You've just exterminated a human life. Is it justified? Probably, but HE didn't crash those planes... He's another Joe who is willing to die for what he believes him. I don't think he's evil or deserving of death because of that. But it happens, and this is a war.<P>||||reg||3
Z||000010||BN Boomer||11-10-2001||01:12 PM||||Godd*mn f*cking right that we're right and they are wrong. We've given quite a bit assistance to Afghanistan and look how they thank us.<P>I can empathize a little with the plight of the average Afghan, but they as a country brought this upon themselves. If they don't like the wrath that their government has brought upon them, maybe they should overthrow it. Otherwise, f*ck 'em.||||reg||1
Z||000011||84fierotrevor||11-10-2001||02:09 PM||||If you think you are totely numb to real vilonce you should see the video I downloaded on morpheus,<BR>I was trying to download that game operation flashpoint and accidintly clicked the one belowe it and saw my downloaded finish in like 4 minutes. I was like um what the hell ?<BR>I clicked open and windows media player opend, and it turned out to be a snuff film of some terrorist with a home camera!<BR>They threw this russion solider about 19-20 years old to the ground,steped on his head and placed a big ass army like knife right threw his throat! and jagidly cut his adams apple out while he gasped and was unable to scream. You would think with all the vilonce on tv and stuff with people having there head blown off, that this wouldn't effect you ether. But it made me jump out of my seat and gag And it made me think about my entire life and just how cruel people in this world are and how stupid war is. and gave me a sick feeling that whole day.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mach10||11-10-2001||02:27 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BN Boomer:<BR><B>Godd*mn f*cking right that we're right and they are wrong. We've given quite a bit assistance to Afghanistan and look how they thank us.<P>I can empathize a little with the plight of the average Afghan, but they as a country brought this upon themselves. If they don't like the wrath that their government has brought upon them, maybe they should overthrow it. Otherwise, f*ck 'em.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I don't think Afghanistan brought it upon themselves. Funny, of all the anti-US protests in the Muslim world, there are the fewest in Afghan... The Afghani people are a very opressed race. They've been in civil war for the past 100 years or so. The Taliban took over the feudal state system that was predominantly in power in 1994. The Taliban has all the guns, and all of the drug-money so they make the rules. The Afghani people have nothing left. They have no weapons. They have little to no rights. They certainly could not stage a political coup at this point. The Taliban rules with an iron fist. These are the people deserving of the wrath of the USAF. So keep up the air-strikes until there is no Taliban left... And the world will be a better place   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 11-10-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000013||BN Boomer||11-10-2001||03:27 PM||||Yeah, and a bunch of rag tag but determined revolutionaries couldn't defeat the most powerful nation in the world, either.<P>And the last time I checked, the Taliban ARE the ones receiving the brunt of the USAFs wrath. Collateral damage may not be pleasant, but it is an unavoidable fact of war.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Mach10||11-10-2001||03:40 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>SHORT BATTLE<P>Northern Alliance Interior Minister Yunis Qanuni told Reuters the local population, mainly ethnic Uzbeks who have little love for the mostly ethnic Pashtun Taliban, aided his forces.<P>``The people of Mazar-i-Sharif helped us. They captured some of the Pakistani and Arab soldiers and handed them over to us,'' he said, referring to foreign fighters of bin Laden's al Qaeda network who are thought to number several thousand in the north.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Give 'em a little help, and they'll do their part too... If you are refering to the New-World vs. England civil wars, You'll find that the US soldiers were not so "rag-tag." Although outnumbered, they did prevail by using slightly (at the time) orthodox methods of warcraft. Guerrilla warfare was considered rather impolite at the time. <P>Your soldiers fought for their freedom: Freedom from the heavy taxation, and freedom from a somewhat oppressive British rule. <P>In Afghanistan, understand that there is little left for these people. They have no weapons, no money, no means of communication. they really ARE living in the stone-age. On the other hand, the Taliban have semi-modern weaponry, SAMs, Machine guns, Tanks, Assault Rifles, Mortars, and Artillery... I can't blame them for not standing up earlier. <P>But, as I said, give 'em a chance, and they will fight too   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Well done!<P>EDIT: Sorry, I didn't mean to imply that the US was targetting anything BUT Taliban forces... My bad  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 11-10-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000015||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-10-2001||04:00 PM||||Anyone else getting more & more annoyed with these Anti-war freaks ragging on the US gov?<P>When I think of how life changed in US/Canada after the WTC, and them taking 5000 innocent people, and think how many kids will grow up not knowing their mother/father due to these towel wearing camel humping freaks.. I feel no pity for them.<P>Maybe the USAF should be dropping Raid on them instead..  thats good for exterminating cockroaches correct?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I think they should be dropping more daisy chain bombs so they can clear the taliban out quicker so everyones lifes can start trying to get back to normal.<BR>(clears a area the size of 5 football fields? sweet!! *on msnbc.com*)<P>||||reg||8
Z||000016||Mach10||11-10-2001||04:32 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by isthiswhereiputausername?:<BR><B>Anyone else getting more & more annoyed with these Anti-war freaks ragging on the US gov?<P>When I think of how life changed in US/Canada after the WTC, and them taking 5000 innocent people, and think how many kids will grow up not knowing their mother/father due to these towel wearing camel humping freaks.. I feel no pity for them.<P>Maybe the USAF should be dropping Raid on them instead..  thats good for exterminating cockroaches correct?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I think they should be dropping more daisy chain bombs so they can clear the taliban out quicker so everyones lifes can start trying to get back to normal.<BR>(clears a area the size of 5 football fields? sweet!! *on msnbc.com*)<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Daisy-Cutter, you'll find   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>No-one's being anti-war... But the US acting out their aggression on civilian targets... That's terrorism. NO BETTER than what happened in NY. <P>At the moment, things are moving nicely. The Taliban is falling like mown grass, and Civilian casualties are minimal. <P>If anyone harbors any resentment to the Afghanis, then you are sadly misguided. Do a little background reading. These people want the Taliban out more than any of the outside countries. Funny... The muslim world views the Taliban as "Freedom Fighters" or "Pure" or "Heroic." Everyone that has DEALINGS with them, (and isn't a terrorist group) thinks that they are a bunch of megalomanic narcissistic oppressive assholes... <P>EDIT: Misread your post, ITWIPAUN  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">... changed things a little. It's not directed at you  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 11-10-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000017||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-10-2001||04:39 PM||||<BR>[/b][/QUOTE]<BR>If anyone harbors any resentment to the Afghanis, then you are sadly misguided. Do a little background reading. These people want the Taliban out more than any of the outside countries. [/B][/QUOTE]<P><BR>I agree.. the afghans and taliban are completely seperate..  <P>Soon as the taliban is "exterminated" maybe life can get back to normal EVERYWHERE in the world (we can only wish it does  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ) ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Mach10||11-10-2001||04:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by isthiswhereiputausername?:<BR><B><BR>I agree.. the afghans and taliban are completely seperate..  <P>Soon as the taliban is "exterminated" maybe life can get back to normal EVERYWHERE in the world (we can only wish it does   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ) </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Personally, I wouldn't mind seeing the Taliban leaders buried up to their ankles in fire-ant hills  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Unfortunately, it won't end there... But it'll be a start... Wouldn't mind seeing a scenario like in Swordfish<P>||||reg||6
Z||000019||maryjane||11-10-2001||06:09 PM||||quote: "These people want the Taliban out more than any ...." Actually, this is an unknown, of sorts. The largest segment of the population is Pashtun, over 58%. Not Islamic fanaticals, they are similar to the Iranian Islamics. They do not approve of the extremes the Taliban has gone to, but neither do they accept a return to the liberal atmosphere the Soviet backed Kabal regime. they are not a military force as we think of them, but do hold the largest land area, in a sparsley populated country. The Pashtun will have to be the primary people to consider in the forming of a new government, once the Taliban has been destroyed. The area outlined in blue approximates the Pashtun population. The Pashtun already consider the attacks to be against them, as they live in the areas that are bearing the brunt of the attacks, namely Khandhar and Kabal. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/af-map.jpg">   <BR><p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 11-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Mach10||11-10-2001||06:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B>quote: "These people want the Taliban out more than any ...." Actually, this is an unknown, of sorts. The largest segment of the population is Pashtun, over 58%. Not Islamic fanaticals, they are similar to the Iranian Islamics. They do not approve of the extremes the Taliban has gone to, but neither do they accept a return to the liberal atmosphere the Soviet backed Kabal regime. they are not a military force as we think of them, but do hold the largest land area, in a sparsley populated country. The Pashtun will have to be the primary people to consider in the forming of a new government, once the Taliban has been destroyed. The area outlined in blue approximates the Pashtun population. The Pashtun already consider the attacks to be against them, as they live in the areas that are bearing the brunt of the attacks, namely Khandhar and Kabal.<P>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 11-10-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is very possible. I am basing my information on a few 3rd party assesment papers (I'd have to look for the links again) that are written on the Taliban's rise to power. The lengths that they are going through to keep the population under their thumbs is suggestive that their initial popularity has pulled a 180, and is going backwards. The Taliban is in DIRE trouble, right now. The Pashtun don't like hefty liberalism, prefering rigid structure. The Taliban is far too bloodthirsty, and they keep changing the rules. This is upsetting people. So, the Taliban has to allocate resources to:<BR>a) Keep their grip on power. They need their military presence to keep order.<BR>b) They are fighting a war with the Alliances<BR>c) They are fighting a war with the US.<P>A desparate man fights a war on 2 fronts. Only a madman attempts 3. <P>I wish them the best of luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif"><P>As far as the population is concerned, I repeat, I am basing this all on assumptions made based on the facts that I have gathered since 9/11. The Taliban's hold grew stronger for the first few years in power, but it is starting to slip. <P>From the point of view of a paleo-anthropologist, I can relate their situation to the Mayan empire. What made them strong at first was a unifying factor (mayan = Worship, Taliban = worship), and a common social goal (mayan = power/wealth, taliban = peace [relative]). After an initial period of exponential growth, the very things that make them (both) strong start to work against them. In the mayan's case, people didn't like being sacrificed/eaten, nor was there enough food, so the society collapsed.<P>For the taliban, the war-torn and weary people got a chance, in relative peace, to re-evaluate their position. Before, there were shells flying everywhere, everyone was busy ducking for cover. The Taliban, with it's greater military force, offered a solution: Peace, by means of greater firepower. The warlords were beaten out of the urban centers, and the Taliban set up shop. Everything was cool. However, no matter WHAT they call themselves, the Taliban IS a military dictatorship. Do what they say or be shot. History has shown that people don't usually tolerate this sort of thing for long. Now, the Taliban, blissfully ignorant of human nature (I really can't think of any other way to explain it) proceeded to up the ante. Start peeling freedoms. No TV, no VCRs, More praying, no drinking. Women suck and should be shot etc... All work and all pray make people something-something....<BR>People are starting to dislike the Taliban, and the Taliban has to push harder to keep things in line. As a result of the harder pushing, people are getting MORE annoyed and starting to push back, resulting in more force. Without US intervention, there would be an uprising or two within 10 years (Mach10 prediction) just given the current stresses.<P>So now, the Big Bad Wolf (USAF) is raining down harsh anal-justice from 35,000ft. Now the people are divided again. "Well, the Taliban do suck, but at least they are only killing INDIVIDUALS. The US is bombing the ****  out of our countryside. We can't read, and we don't get TV, so we don't really know WHY the US are doing this. The only source we have is fighting them, and spreading all sorts of  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"> about what's happening. But it's all I have to work with..." This is again polarizing the country. Some just want the US to leave them alone. Others share the views of the Taliban. Still others want the Taliban GONE. Others don't care, they just want to herd their damned sheep.<P>So yeah, it's a tough call. I stick by the "They want 'em out" view. Be be sure, they don't want the US in there, either... But it appears that they'd be happier without the Taliban killing their women.<P>||||reg||6
Z||000021||maryjane||11-10-2001||07:03 PM||||"Now the big bad wolf , the USAF is raining down harsh anal justice from 35,000ft" Reminds me of the email I got. Put all the rubble from the WTC, huge concrete chunks, steel, appliances, desks, in C-130's & C-5's and drop it all over Afganistan from 30,000ft. A Fridgedaire can do a lot of damage from that high up. Headlines would read: WORLD TRADE CENTER STRIKES BACK!! We could claim it's humanitarian, lend-lease donations. For that matter, we could divert all U.S. mail for a week and drop it all over the country. They'd be so afraid of getting anthrax, they'd surrender in a week. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">:||||reg||1
